.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/@tailwindcss/typography Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
\fR  \fP 

.PP
The official Tailwind CSS Typography plugin provides a set of \fRprose\fP classes you can use to add beautiful typographic defaults to any vanilla HTML you don’t control, like HTML rendered from Markdown, or pulled from a CMS\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose lg:prose\-xl">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
To see what it looks like in action, check out our \fRlive demo\fP on Tailwind Play\&.

.PP
.PP
.SS "Installation"
Install the plugin from npm:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install \-D @tailwindcss/typography
.fi
.PP

.PP
Then add the plugin to your main \fRstyle\&.css\fP file:

.PP
.PP
.nf
  @import "tailwindcss";
+ @plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";
.fi
.PP

.PP
If you are still using \fBTailwind CSS v3\fP, add the plugin to your \fRtailwind\&.config\&.js\fP file:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// tailwind\&.config\&.js
module\&.exports = {
  theme: {
    // \&.\&.\&.
  },
  plugins: [
    require('@tailwindcss/typography'),
    // \&.\&.\&.
  ],
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.SS "Basic usage"
Now you can use the \fRprose\fP classes to add sensible typography styles to any vanilla HTML:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose lg:prose\-xl">
  <h1>Garlic bread with cheese: What the science tells us</h1>
  <p>
    For years parents have espoused the health benefits of eating garlic bread with cheese to their
    children, with the food earning such an iconic status in our culture that kids will often dress
    up as warm, cheesy loaf for Halloween\&.
  </p>
  <p>
    But a recent study shows that the celebrated appetizer may be linked to a series of rabies cases
    springing up around the country\&.
  </p>
  <!\-\- \&.\&.\&. \-\->
</article>
.fi
.PP
.SS "Choosing a gray scale"
This plugin includes a modifier class for each of the five gray scales Tailwind includes by default so you can easily style your content to match the grays you're using in your project\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose prose\-slate">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Here are the classes that are generated using a totally default Tailwind CSS v2\&.0 build:

.PP
Class  Gray scale  \fRprose-gray\fP \fI(default)\fP  Gray  \fRprose-slate\fP  Slate  \fRprose-zinc\fP  Zinc  \fRprose-neutral\fP  Neutral  \fRprose-stone\fP  Stone  

.PP
Modifier classes are designed to be used with the \fRmulti-class modifier pattern\fP and must be used in conjunction with the base \fRprose\fP class\&.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
Always include the \fRprose\fP class when adding a gray scale modifier
.RE
.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose prose\-stone">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
To learn about creating your own color themes, read the \fBadding custom color themes\fP documentation\&.
.SS "Applying a type scale"
Size modifiers allow you to adjust the overall size of your typography for different contexts\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose prose\-xl">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Five different typography sizes are included out of the box:

.PP
Class  Body font size  \fRprose-sm\fP  0\&.875rem \fI(14px)\fP  \fRprose-base\fP \fI(default)\fP  1rem \fI(16px)\fP  \fRprose-lg\fP  1\&.125rem \fI(18px)\fP  \fRprose-xl\fP  1\&.25rem \fI(20px)\fP  \fRprose-2xl\fP  1\&.5rem \fI(24px)\fP  

.PP
These can be used in combination with Tailwind's \fRbreakpoint modifiers\fP to change the overall font size of a piece of content at different viewport sizes:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose md:prose\-lg lg:prose\-xl">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Everything about the provided size modifiers has been hand-tuned by professional designers to look as beautiful as possible, including the relationships between font sizes, heading spacing, code block padding, and more\&.

.PP
Size modifiers are designed to be used with the \fRmulti-class modifier pattern\fP and must be used in conjunction with the base \fRprose\fP class\&.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
Always include the \fRprose\fP class when adding a size modifier
.RE
.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose prose\-lg">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
To learn about customizing the included type scales, read the documentation on \fBcustomizing the CSS\fP\&.
.SS "Adapting to dark mode"
Each default color theme includes a hand-designed dark mode version that you can trigger by adding the \fRprose-invert\fP class:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose dark:prose\-invert">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
To learn about creating your own color themes, read the \fBadding custom color themes\fP documentation\&.
.SS "Element modifiers"
Use element modifiers to customize the style of individual elements in your content directly in your HTML:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose prose\-img:rounded\-xl prose\-headings:underline prose\-a:text\-blue\-600">
  {{ markdown }}
</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
This makes it easy to do things like style links to match your brand, add a border radius to images, and tons more\&.

.PP
Here's a complete list of available element modifiers:

.PP
Modifier  Target  \fRprose-headings:{utility}\fP  \fRh1\fP, \fRh2\fP, \fRh3\fP, \fRh4\fP, \fRth\fP  \fRprose-lead:{utility}\fP  \fR[class~="lead"]\fP  \fRprose-h1:{utility}\fP  \fRh1\fP  \fRprose-h2:{utility}\fP  \fRh2\fP  \fRprose-h3:{utility}\fP  \fRh3\fP  \fRprose-h4:{utility}\fP  \fRh4\fP  \fRprose-p:{utility}\fP  \fRp\fP  \fRprose-a:{utility}\fP  \fRa\fP  \fRprose-blockquote:{utility}\fP  \fRblockquote\fP  \fRprose-figure:{utility}\fP  \fRfigure\fP  \fRprose-figcaption:{utility}\fP  \fRfigcaption\fP  \fRprose-strong:{utility}\fP  \fRstrong\fP  \fRprose-em:{utility}\fP  \fRem\fP  \fRprose-kbd:{utility}\fP  \fRkbd\fP  \fRprose-code:{utility}\fP  \fRcode\fP  \fRprose-pre:{utility}\fP  \fRpre\fP  \fRprose-ol:{utility}\fP  \fRol\fP  \fRprose-ul:{utility}\fP  \fRul\fP  \fRprose-li:{utility}\fP  \fRli\fP  \fRprose-dl:{utility}\fP  \fRdl\fP  \fRprose-dt:{utility}\fP  \fRdt\fP  \fRprose-dd:{utiddty}\fP  \fRdd\fP  \fRprose-table:{utility}\fP  \fRtable\fP  \fRprose-thead:{utility}\fP  \fRthead\fP  \fRprose-tr:{utility}\fP  \fRtr\fP  \fRprose-th:{utility}\fP  \fRth\fP  \fRprose-td:{utility}\fP  \fRtd\fP  \fRprose-img:{utility}\fP  \fRimg\fP  \fRprose-picture:{utility}\fP  \fRpicture\fP  \fRprose-video:{utility}\fP  \fRvideo\fP  \fRprose-hr:{utility}\fP  \fRhr\fP  

.PP
When stacking these modifiers with other modifiers like \fRhover\fP, you most likely want the other modifier to come last:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose prose\-a:text\-blue\-600 prose\-a:hover:text\-blue\-500">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
If you are still using in Tailwind CSS v3, the modifier order is the opposite:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose prose\-a:text\-blue\-600 hover:prose\-a:text\-blue\-500">{{ markdown }}</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Read the Tailwind CSS documentation on \fRstacked modifiers\fP to learn more\&.
.SS "Overriding max-width"
Each size modifier comes with a baked in \fRmax-width\fP designed to keep the content as readable as possible\&. This isn't always what you want though, and sometimes you'll want the content to just fill the width of its container\&.

.PP
In those cases, all you need to do is add \fRmax-w-none\fP to your content to override the embedded max-width:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<div class="grid grid\-cols\-4">
  <div class="col\-span\-1">
    <!\-\- \&.\&.\&. \-\->
  </div>
  <div class="col\-span\-3">
    <article class="prose max\-w\-none">{{ markdown }}</article>
  </div>
</div>
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.SS "Advanced topics"
.SS "Undoing typography styles"
If you have a block of markup embedded in some content that shouldn't inherit the \fRprose\fP styles, use the \fRnot-prose\fP class to sandbox it:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<article class="prose">
  <h1>My Heading</h1>
  <p>\&.\&.\&.</p>

  <div class="not\-prose">
    <!\-\- Some example or demo that needs to be prose\-free \-\->
  </div>

  <p>\&.\&.\&.</p>
  <!\-\- \&.\&.\&. \-\->
</article>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Note that you can't nest new \fRprose\fP instances within a \fRnot-prose\fP block at this time\&.

.PP
Even when using a prefix for your utilities \fRnot-prose\fP should not have a prefix\&.
.SS "Adding custom color themes"
To customize the color theme beyond simple CSS overrides, add a \fR@utility\fP directive to your CSS file:

.PP
.PP
.nf
@utility prose\-pink {
  \-\-tw\-prose\-body: var(\-\-color\-pink\-800);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-headings: var(\-\-color\-pink\-900);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-lead: var(\-\-color\-pink\-700);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-links: var(\-\-color\-pink\-900);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-bold: var(\-\-color\-pink\-900);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-counters: var(\-\-color\-pink\-600);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-bullets: var(\-\-color\-pink\-400);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-hr: var(\-\-color\-pink\-300);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-quotes: var(\-\-color\-pink\-900);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-quote\-borders: var(\-\-color\-pink\-300);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-captions: var(\-\-color\-pink\-700);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-code: var(\-\-color\-pink\-900);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-pre\-code: var(\-\-color\-pink\-100);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-pre\-bg: var(\-\-color\-pink\-900);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-th\-borders: var(\-\-color\-pink\-300);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-td\-borders: var(\-\-color\-pink\-200);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-body: var(\-\-color\-pink\-200);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-headings: var(\-\-color\-white);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-lead: var(\-\-color\-pink\-300);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-links: var(\-\-color\-white);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-bold: var(\-\-color\-white);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-counters: var(\-\-color\-pink\-400);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-bullets: var(\-\-color\-pink\-600);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-hr: var(\-\-color\-pink\-700);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-quotes: var(\-\-color\-pink\-100);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-quote\-borders: var(\-\-color\-pink\-700);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-captions: var(\-\-color\-pink\-400);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-code: var(\-\-color\-white);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-pre\-code: var(\-\-color\-pink\-300);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-pre\-bg: rgb(0 0 0 / 50%);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-th\-borders: var(\-\-color\-pink\-600);
  \-\-tw\-prose\-invert\-td\-borders: var(\-\-color\-pink\-700);
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
